WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.800
One hundred years ago, a reclusive Czech-Jew
living anonymously amid Prague’s spires

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.180
managed to change the way we see the world.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.300
He was the son of a shopkeeper, an insurance
lawyer by trade.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.870
Outwardly, he appeared sickly, forgettable.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:14.059
But inside?

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:20.250
Inside, this sallow young man’s mind was
alive: with visions of horror, with paranoia,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.009
existential dread, and alienation.

00:00:23.009 --> 00:00:28.210
Working at night, he took those visions and
etched them onto paper, creating fiction unlike

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.000
anything ever seen before.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.630
That young man’s name was Franz Kafka.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:41.949
And his work would shape the entire 20th Century.

00:00:41.949 --> 00:00:46.140
Born into a German-speaking family in the
Austro-Hungarian Empire, Kafka spent his whole

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.530
life suffering from illness and anxiety.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:53.039
He was miserable at home, unlucky in love,
and too depressed to function anywhere but

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:54.420
in his insurance office.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:00.789
Yet he made up for this by having a mind so
strange, so inventive that today he’s arguably

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:02.880
Europe’s most famous author.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:09.890
Join us as we delve inside the work of Franz
Kafka, and the awful life that made it all

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:10.890
possible.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:17.630
Early Years: In the Penal Colony
It was a hot July day in 1883 when Hermann

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:19.710
Kafka became a father for the first time.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:24.369
A Prague shopkeeper from a working class Jewish
family, Hermann was a man who’d fought all

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:26.210
his life to get where he was.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.060
His battle had been a tough one.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:33.579
1883 was the era of the Austro-Hungarian Empire,
when Prague was dominated by Germans, and

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:35.990
Jews were at a constant disadvantage.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.159
Through the sheer force of his gigantic, volatile
personality, Hermann had managed to take a

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:43.450
little clothing shop and turn it into a bustling
store.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:48.950
He’d bought a fancy house, found an educated
wife, even broken into the closed world of

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:49.950
the German elites.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.820
And now his first son had arrived.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:56.920
The son who was going to be as sporty, as
commanding, as powerful, and as straightforwardly

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.509
Germanic as his old man affected to be.

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:04.530
It’s likely that, as Hermann took baby Franz
Kafka into his arms for the first time, he

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.630
felt a rush of pride.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:11.020
If he did, it was the last time he ever felt
anything but contempt for his son.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.470
From the earliest age, it was obvious that
Franz Kafka was not the stout, hearty man

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.720
Hermann wanted him to be.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.440
The boy was small, sickly.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:22.230
He was troubled by boils, and afflicted with
melancholy.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.690
From Hermann’s perspective, this was a little
like opening a box you think is going to contain

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:30.740
a delicious cake, only to discover it instead
contains bitter disappointment.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:35.760
But from Franz Kafka’s perspective, it was
like being born into a world where everything

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.660
you did was doomed to end in humiliation.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:43.280
Hermann was an angry man at the best of times,
but something about his ineffectual son seems

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.030
to have actively offended him.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.010
He would explode at young Kafka for no reason.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:52.270
Belittle him endlessly for being useless,
pathetic, a failure of a human.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:57.530
Perhaps its not surprising that, by the time
Kafka started school, his self-esteem was

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:58.630
in the gutter.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:02.200
Still, while he may have done everything in
his power to destroy his son’s confidence

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.960
at home, Hermann was still, at heart, a social
climber.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:10.630
That meant making sure that, outwardly, Kafka
appeared to have every advantage in life.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:15.950
As Hermann’s business boomed, he began sending
Kafka to the elite Altstädter Staatsgymnasium,

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.990
having one of the family’s Czech servants
walk the lad there every morning.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:23.710
For the working class Jew, it must’ve been
a whole peacock’s worth of feathers in his

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.720
cap to send his boy somewhere so prestigious.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.680
But for his shy, miserable son, it was simply
Hell.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:31.920
Kafka hated the school.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.320
Absolutely hated it.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.240
While he did well academically, he found the
place authoritarian, dehumanizing, alienating.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:43.700
It was around this time that a black parasite
first began to tunnel its way into the boy’s

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:44.700
brain.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.860
That parasite was what we’d today call a
serious anxiety disorder.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:54.470
One that made Kafka constantly feel like there
was a ball of wire at the center of his body,

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:55.630
winding ever tighter.

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:59.722
If you’ve ever suffered from anxiety, you’ll
know that “suffering” is the exact right

00:03:59.722 --> 00:04:01.010
term to use.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.070
Well Franz Kafka would suffer from it for
the rest of his life.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.970
However, the boy’s teenage years weren’t
all bad news.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:12.040
Between the emotional abuse at home and his
miserable life at school, Kafka needed an

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.830
outlet, somewhere he could escape to.

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.920
A warm burrow he could hide in.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.989
It was at this time that the boy started writing.

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:27.810
Description of a Struggle
Come 1902, Franz Kafka was studying law at

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.220
Charles University, and writing on the side.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:34.190
He’d actually wanted to study chemistry,
but had switched to law after two weeks when

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.560
Hermann pressured him, because law would look
better to the family’s middle class friends.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.190
And with his son already a self-declared atheist
and socialist, Hermann desperately needed

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:47.160
a new feather for his suddenly bedraggled-looking
cap.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.730
Yet even with his father still controlling
his life at university, Kafka managed to eke

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:53.960
out some space for himself.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:59.930
He joined Lese und Redehalle der Deutschen
Studenten, a literary club for German speakers.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:03.740
While at this club, he met the man who would
one day come to define Kafka’s legacy.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:05.390
That man was Max Brod.

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:10.110
Born about a year after Kafka, Max Brod was
an outgoing Jewish intellectual who was charming,

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:15.020
creative, intelligent, and everything Kafka
felt he himself was not.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:18.490
Nonetheless, the two hit it off, forming their
own little clique.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:22.570
It would be this close friendship that later
saved Kafka’s writing from the ashes.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:23.730
But not yet.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.810
Brod and Kafka were both just students at
this time, a long way from actually publishing

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:28.810
their stories.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:33.780
So when Kafka finally graduated in June, 1906,
it was not into a life of writing and plumbing

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.710
the depths of the human soul, but into a life
of working as an unpaid clerk for the local

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:39.710
courts.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:44.510
To say Kafka found this job crushingly dull
would be to overestimate how exciting it was.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.700
The work left him cranky, depressed, and lethargic.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:52.930
Worse, he was still living at home, still
dealing with Hermann’s volcanic temper and

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:53.930
tiny cruelties.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.570
His only solace was that soon it would be
over.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:00.770
Soon, Kafka would have enough experience to
join an international office, one that would

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:03.090
send him abroad, away from his father.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:08.120
In October, 1907, Kafka finally signed up
with an Italian insurance company, fully expecting

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.389
to be sent down to Italy at any day.

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:15.080
Instead, he found himself stuck in a Prague
office, dealing with suffocating bureaucracy,

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:19.580
unpaid overtime, and brutal hours that left
him too exhausted to write.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:24.510
By the time Kafka finally resigned, his dream
of living abroad had been crushed.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:29.310
Instead, he meekly resigned himself to staying
at home, to living in Prague and sharing a

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:34.020
house with Hermann, to living with his father’s
constant putdowns.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:38.510
In August, 1908, Kafka began his new job at
the Worker’s Accident Insurance Institute

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.330
in the shadow of Prague Castle.

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:43.460
He would stay there for the rest of his working
life.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:48.400
But even as Kafka’s dream of leaving his
abuser far behind faded into distant memory,

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.130
he began to nurture another.

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.560
Kafka’s new job finished in the mid-afternoon.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.620
If he slept after it finished, that meant
he could wake up after dark and spend most

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:57.620
of the night writing.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:03.950
Kafka would later say this routine felt less
like a calling or a hobby than a form of communion.

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:08.930
Maybe even a penance, with writing being the
only way he could atone for the sins he was

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:12.210
convinced filled his stick-thin body.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:16.610
That same year, Max Brod convinced Kafka to
publish his first stories.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:20.740
Although they sold poorly, the critical reaction
was good enough to convince Kafka to keep

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.040
writing.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:30.080
The Metamorphosis
In August, 1912, Kafka arrived at a dinner

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.389
party in Max Brod’s home to find his friend
had an unexpected guest.

00:07:34.389 --> 00:07:38.650
It was now four years since Kafka had published
his first collection of stories, and he was

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:44.380
waist deep in the novel that would later become
Amerika, AKA The Man Who Disappeared.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:48.710
But that night, literature was the furthest
thing from Kafka’s mind.

00:07:48.710 --> 00:07:51.900
He was too busy thinking about the woman sat
opposite him.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:57.200
Felice Bauer was one of those “friends of
a friend of a cousin” types that the gregarious

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:01.880
Brod would invite to his home, a woman who
usually lived in Berlin but was visiting Prague.

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.940
In his diary, Kafka would later write that
she had a:

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:07.660
“bony, empty face that wore its emptiness
openly.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:08.740
Bare throat.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:09.980
A blouse thrown on...

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:11.230
Almost broken nose.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:14.900
Blonde, somewhat straight, unattractive hair,
strong chin.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:19.590
As I was taking my seat I looked at her closely
for the first time, by the time I was seated

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:22.730
I already had an unshakeable opinion.”

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.430
That unshakeable opinion was that he was in
love.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:30.139
This being Franz Kafka, the courtship that
started that summer evening wasn’t exactly

00:08:30.139 --> 00:08:31.139
conventional.

00:08:31.139 --> 00:08:34.580
He waited until Felice Bauer had gone back
to Berlin before telling her of his feelings

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.580
via letter.

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:39.280
From then on, the two were writing to one
another two times a day.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:41.320
Or Kafka was, at any rate.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:46.620
For her part, Bauer seems to have been flattered,
but maybe not quite to the extent of writing

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.050
long, heart-rending letters every few hours.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:51.839
Still, her effect on the writer was palpable.

00:08:51.839 --> 00:08:55.890
Not six weeks after they’d met, Kafka sat
down one evening at his desk overlooking the

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.120
Vltava River.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.310
By the time he got up again, it was dawn,
his legs were in agony, and he’d just finished

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:02.880
his first masterpiece.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.740
The Judgement is a hard tale to describe,
in that it’s a typically Kafka short story

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.390
without an easy meaning.

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:12.880
The basic plot is that a young man goes to
tend to his sick father.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:18.760
But as the tale progresses, the father grows
in stature and power until, like some terrible,

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:23.080
tyrant king, he rises from his bed and condemns
the young man to death by drowning.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:26.550
The man immediately goes and throws himself
into the Vltava.

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:31.520
Gee, we wonder where your son got the inspiration
for that tale, Hermann.

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:37.730
Yet if the tale sounds slight in our description,
actually reading it is a whole other experience.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:43.270
The tale is darkly humorous, almost allegorical,
but at the same time shot through with a sense

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:47.700
of grander themes lurking beneath the surface,
just out of reach.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.700
For Kafka, it was the first good thing he
ever wrote, the moment when he metamorphosed

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.930
from a man who did some writing to an actual
writer.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:10:00.040
That same year, he would sit down and write
a much more literal tale of metamorphosis,

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:05.870
about Gregor Samsa, who woke up one morning
from uneasy dreams to find himself changed

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:08.690
in his bed into a monstrous vermin.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:13.420
But while The Metamorphosis would be the only
work in Kafka’s lifetime that sold well,

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:18.430
and would remain his best-known story, it
was The Judgement that the author considered

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:19.670
his masterpiece.

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:22.540
At the top of the first page, he scrawled
a hasty dedication.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:27.680
“To Miss Felice Bauer.”

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:33.080
A Little Woman
When you look at this period of Franz Kafka’s

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:39.250
life, it’s remarkable just how closely his
work parallels his relationship with Bauer.

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:42.300
Remarkable, because the two barely ever met.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:46.710
Although they constantly sent one another
letters, Kafka constantly turned down chances

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:49.680
to meet his “beloved” in the flesh.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.240
He was too busy at work.

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:52.960
His writing required attention.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.440
He had a headache and was washing his hair.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.570
It’s been suggested the paradox at the heart
of Kafka’s life is that he thought he wanted

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:04.830
to be left completely alone, but secretly
craved human contact.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:06.730
Or maybe the other way around.

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:11.290
Still, he evidently longed for company enough
to eventually propose to Bauer.

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:12.600
By letter, naturally.

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:18.050
On May 31, 1914, the couple finally met face
to face again at their engagement party.

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:23.450
Surrounded by his family and Felice’s, Kafka
felt nothing but bottomless misery.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:27.480
Before three months were out, the two had
mutually broken off their engagement.

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:32.360
Although they’d wind up getting back together,
you can see clearly the effect this rollercoaster

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.490
had on Kafka’s work.

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:39.130
1914 was the year he abandoned Amerika with
just one chapter left to write.

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:44.160
It’s the year he wrote In the Penal Colony,
a grim, cruel story that’s even darker than

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:45.160
The Judgement.

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:49.370
It’s also the year he started his most famous
novel of all: The Trial.

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:53.980
The story of a man, Joseph K, who is charged
with a crime without ever knowing what that

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:58.360
crime is, The Trial is a landmark of absurd,
alienated fiction.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:02.970
For chapter after chapter, Joseph K tries
desperately to find out what he’s guilty

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:08.620
of, while uncovering all sorts of bizarre
figures, grotesque tortures, and strange sexualized

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:10.680
rituals happening in secret rooms.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.050
By the end, we still don’t know what crime
he committed.

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:18.650
Because it’s so ambiguous, The Trial has
come to symbolize a million things to a million

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:19.650
readers.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:22.130
For some, Joseph K’s crime is simply that
he’s human.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:26.050
For others, it’s that he’s a Jew living
in an anti-Semitic world.

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:29.950
Others see in it a premonition of the Soviet
Union’s secret police.

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:34.780
In the 1960s, Orson Welles made a film version
that hinted Joseph K was targeted for being

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:35.780
gay.

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:40.860
In short, The Trial is one of those books
that’s so universal everyone can see their

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:42.500
experiences reflected in it.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:44.300
But not during Kafka’s lifetime.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:49.080
In 1915, the reclusive writer slipped his
manuscript of The Trial into a desk drawer.

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:50.930
He never looked at it again.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:55.020
The following year, Kafka and Felice Bauer
spent nearly two weeks in the spa town of

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:57.330
Marienbad, attempting to patch things up.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:02.130
It worked and, on July 12, 1917, the two got
engaged all over again.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:06.000
This time they even started making plans to
live together in Prague.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:10.760
But 1917 was also the year Franz Kafka got
some life changing news.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:13.920
Or maybe life ending might be more accurate.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:17.510
That year, he was diagnosed with tuberculosis.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:21.650
In the early years of the 20th Century, tuberculosis
was a death sentence.

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:26.050
The disease killed Andrew Jackson, Jane Austen,
George Orwell, and was once estimated to have

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:29.930
killed 15% of all people who had ever lived.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:31.760
And it would kill Franz Kafka too.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:37.730
In December, 1917, a sick and depressed Kafka
broke off his engagement to Felice for a second

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:38.730
time.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:40.110
For Felice, this was the last straw.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:44.830
She cut off contact with her former fiancee,
found a more stable man to marry.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:51.230
The part of Kafka that wished he could be
completely alone had gotten what it wanted.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:58.520
Kafka’s Judgement
On October 28, 1918, the Austro-Hungarian

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:01.900
Empire gave one last groan and collapsed.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:07.000
As WWI drew to a close, Prague found itself
no longer under Germanic rule, but part of

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:10.050
a new republic composed of Czechs and Slovaks.

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:15.010
At his workplace, Kafka was taken for questioning
along with all the other German speakers to

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:17.480
see if they supported the new regime.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:19.850
Those who were actively against it were fired.

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:24.210
But we can only imagine that Kafka didn’t
care all that much about that stuff.

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:27.150
He had more important things to worry about.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:30.500
In early 1919, Kafka was taking some time
away from Prague in a boarding house when

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:32.580
he happened to meet Julie Wohryzek.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:35.640
At only 28, she was nearly 8 years his junior.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:40.920
But still, the two fell to talking, and bonded
over a shared love of Yiddish theatre and

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.120
risque humor.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:45.680
By the time they both returned to Prague,
Kafka had decided to marry her.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:50.680
As firm bases for a lasting relationship go,
there are probably better ones.

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:55.110
But still, Wohryzek agreed, and an excited
Kafka ran to tell his parents.

00:14:55.110 --> 00:15:00.430
At which point Hermann Kafka detonated like
a pink, flabby bomb.

00:15:00.430 --> 00:15:04.660
If Hermann disapproved of his useless son
in general, then you’d have to invent whole

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:10.240
new ways of measuring disapproval to understand
how he felt about this whirlwind marriage.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:13.390
For the next few months, Kafka’s father
harangued him.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:14.390
Bullied him.

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:15.640
Browbeat him over his plans.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:17.940
At first, Kafka tried to stand his ground.

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:22.149
But a lifetime of belittling had crushed his
self esteem down to nothing.

00:15:22.149 --> 00:15:25.190
In November, 1919, Kafka broke off his engagement
with Wohryzek.

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:29.890
Not long after, Kafka left Prague again and
checked himself into the same guesthouse where

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:31.620
he’d met his second fiancee.

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:35.980
There, he sat down and composed a letter to
his father, finally saying all the things

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:38.300
he was too scared to say to his face.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:43.480
Running at 45 pages, Kafka’s Letter to His
Father was his own judgement on the tyrant

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:44.480
who’d raised him.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:47.930
What’s remarkable today is just how clear-eyed
it was.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:52.730
Kafka was at pains to stress he understood
his father’s position; that he knew dragging

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:56.560
yourself up from a working class background
was not an easy thing to do.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:00.600
While Hermann may be the villain in our story
today, it’s worth remembering that no-one

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.750
in real life is purely evil.

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:07.350
Hermann Kafka was just a man who’d been
taught by the snobbish society he lived in

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:13.080
that only being loud, brash, and angry would
get him noticed, get him respected.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:18.060
It’s just a shame he felt those qualities
should apply to his parenting, too.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:21.740
At the end of the letter, Kafka wrote that
he hoped his honesty would open the door to

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:24.279
an improved relationship between the two of
them.

00:16:24.279 --> 00:16:29.200
But then, shy and insecure to the last, he
chickened out of posting it.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:33.770
The next year, possibly as a rebound, Kafka
started a relationship with one of his translators

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:37.089
in Vienna, a Czech girl called Milena Jesenska.

00:16:37.089 --> 00:16:41.490
But when November again rolled around, Kafka
abruptly broke off the relationship.

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:45.779
Once again, the twisted version of Hermann
sitting in the center of Kafka’s mind had

00:16:45.779 --> 00:16:49.340
condemned the writer to a living death.

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:57.050
A Hunger Artist
Kurt Vonnegut once wrote that Kafka’s stories

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:01.970
exemplify a certain type of fiction, in which
the protagonist starts miserable, only to

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:04.490
see things get continuously worse.

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:06.709
The same was true of Kafka’s life.

00:17:06.709 --> 00:17:10.319
Fair warning: there’s going to be no heroic
moment in this tale.

00:17:10.319 --> 00:17:16.120
No emotional climax where Kafka manages to
finally stand up to Hermann, get the girl,

00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:18.380
and ride off into the Prague sunset.

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:23.620
No, instead, we’re going to watch a miserable
man’s life get continuously worse, until

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:26.429
it all ends the only way it can.

00:17:26.429 --> 00:17:30.009
By 1922, it was obvious what that ending would
be.

00:17:30.009 --> 00:17:34.539
That year, Kafka’s tuberculosis got so bad
he was finally forced to retire from work.

00:17:34.539 --> 00:17:36.860
In one sense, this was actually a good thing.

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:41.090
Kafka had long wished he could devote himself
to writing full time, and now there was nothing

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:42.090
stopping him.

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:46.179
That January, he began writing his last great
work, The Castle.

00:17:46.179 --> 00:17:51.049
The story of a man known only as K, The Castle
follows K’s attempts to meet the authorities

00:17:51.049 --> 00:17:56.659
presiding over a snowbound town, only to discover
his path blocked at every turn.

00:17:56.659 --> 00:17:59.840
Today, The Castle is seen as Kafka’s most
mature novel.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:02.429
But Kafka himself didn’t feel this way.

00:18:02.429 --> 00:18:07.299
On September 11, 1922, Kafka wrote to Max
Brod to inform him he’d given up on the

00:18:07.299 --> 00:18:09.350
story and would never return to it.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:12.859
Yet it wouldn’t be fair to say Kafka had
also given up on life.

00:18:12.859 --> 00:18:15.109
The next year, the sick man moved to Berlin.

00:18:15.109 --> 00:18:20.220
One day, he booked himself a trip to the resort
town of Muritz to try and help his TB.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:23.299
It was while on the shore that Kafka met Dora
Diamant.

00:18:23.299 --> 00:18:27.359
A 19-year old socialist, Dora was 20 years
Kafka’s junior.

00:18:27.359 --> 00:18:31.669
But she was also fiercely intelligent, with
hard-edged political beliefs, and a passionate

00:18:31.669 --> 00:18:35.409
support for Zionism that chimed with Kafka’s
own feelings.

00:18:35.409 --> 00:18:40.220
At Muritz, the two talked and talked until,
finally, it seemed only natural that Dora

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:42.029
would return to Berlin with Kafka.

00:18:42.029 --> 00:18:44.880
But don’t assume this was some great autumn
romance.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:50.470
By 1923, Kafka was so sick that Dora’s role
was more that of a live-in nurse than a lover.

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:52.610
He was also stony broke.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:56.169
Hyperinflation had gripped Weimar Germany,
wiping out Kafka’s pension.

00:18:56.169 --> 00:19:01.289
By November, 1923, the pair were reduced to
using candles to heat their food.

00:19:01.289 --> 00:19:06.270
Still, Dora’s influence was causing Kafka
to feel a tiny ray of optimism for the first

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:07.270
time in years.

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:10.460
He even began making plans to move to Palestine.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:12.669
But this is the story of Franz Kafka.

00:19:12.669 --> 00:19:16.840
And there was no way fate was going to grant
him such happiness.

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:20.309
In early 1924, Kafka’s health finally collapsed.

00:19:20.309 --> 00:19:25.230
Forced by his doctor to check into a hospital,
Kafka selected a sanitorium outside Vienna.

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:27.960
Then, gloomily, he left Berlin for good.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:32.830
As he made his way towards his final resting
place, Kafka found time to stop in Prague.

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:37.360
Just long enough to give Max Brod his unfinished
manuscripts of The Trial, Amerika, and The

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:39.869
Castle and ask him to burn them.

00:19:39.869 --> 00:19:44.159
In late March, 1924, Kafka arrived at the
Vienna sanitorium for treatment.

00:19:44.159 --> 00:19:47.639
Three months later, on June 3, he died of
his illness.

00:19:47.639 --> 00:19:51.749
On his deathbed, he wried Dora Diamant’s
father, asking for permission to marry her.

00:19:51.749 --> 00:19:54.350
Dora’s father curtly replied “no.”

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:59.600
At the time he passed away, Kafka was a man
whose life story seemed to be that of a sickly

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:03.789
insurance lawyer who was unlucky in love,
and never did anything of note.

00:20:03.789 --> 00:20:07.929
His writing was known only by a tiny circle
of Prague intellectuals, and even they’d

00:20:07.929 --> 00:20:09.720
only seen the published stuff.

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:14.169
The bulk of his work had been seen only by
Kafka, who died having made his best friend

00:20:14.169 --> 00:20:15.409
promise to burn it all.

00:20:15.409 --> 00:20:21.239
Luckily for posterity, that best friend just
happened to be a consummate liar.

00:20:21.239 --> 00:20:28.570
The Man Who Re-Appeared
Many years later, Max Brod would reflect on

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:32.299
his final meeting with Kafka, and offer this
explanation.

00:20:32.299 --> 00:20:35.940
According to Brod, Kafka knew his best friend
would never burn his manuscripts.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:39.860
If he’d really wanted them destroyed, he
would’ve asked Dora or Hermann, who’d

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:42.009
have probably burned them with relish.

00:20:42.009 --> 00:20:47.779
By asking Brod, Kafka was in effect asking
the one person he knew would ignore his wishes

00:20:47.779 --> 00:20:49.610
and try to publish his novels.

00:20:49.610 --> 00:20:51.250
And that’s exactly what Brod did.

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:56.230
All through the rest of 1924, Brod diligently
organized Kafka’s papers, sifting the piles

00:20:56.230 --> 00:21:01.289
of notes into chapters, grouping them by theme,
figuring out how they might slot into a wider

00:21:01.289 --> 00:21:02.289
novel.

00:21:02.289 --> 00:21:04.549
By early 1925, he was finished.

00:21:04.549 --> 00:21:07.869
That April, Brod used his literary connections
to get The Trial published.

00:21:07.869 --> 00:21:12.100
It was well enough received in Europe that
The Castle followed a year later, and Amerika

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:13.440
a year after that.

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:18.479
By 1930, all three of Kafka’s novels, plus
his collected short stories, were available

00:21:18.479 --> 00:21:20.820
for European intellectuals to marvel over.

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:24.700
Come 1937, the first English editions even
began to appear.

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:28.499
But it would be another decade before the
spark really lit.

00:21:28.499 --> 00:21:33.649
After WWII, publishing houses in America began
to take a chance on this long-dead author

00:21:33.649 --> 00:21:34.649
they were hearing so much about.

00:21:34.649 --> 00:21:35.649
It was this more than anything that cemented
Kafka’s reputation.

00:21:35.649 --> 00:21:36.649
For whatever reason, postwar America was primed
and ready for Kafka.

00:21:36.649 --> 00:21:37.649
His popularity exploded.

00:21:37.649 --> 00:21:38.649
People like Nabokov sang his praises.

00:21:38.649 --> 00:21:40.551
Below the border, southern American writers
like Gabriel Garcia Marquez and Jorge Luis

00:21:40.551 --> 00:21:42.239
Borges devoured his works.

00:21:42.239 --> 00:21:44.899
The rest, as they say, is history.

00:21:44.899 --> 00:21:49.450
Today, Kafka is one of a handful of writers
almost everyone has heard of, an author as

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:53.389
recognizable as George Orwell, Edgar Allen
Poe, or H.P.

00:21:53.389 --> 00:21:54.389
Lovecraft.

00:21:54.389 --> 00:21:59.159
But while all these men were known as writers
in their day - even the underappreciated Lovecraft

00:21:59.159 --> 00:22:05.809
- Kafka is maybe the only one who rose from
complete obscurity, and brought such complex,

00:22:05.809 --> 00:22:07.039
unique riches with him.

00:22:07.039 --> 00:22:11.820
If you go to Prague’s new Jewish cemetery
today, you can still see the shared grave

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:16.330
where Kafka and Hermann lie for eternity,
side by side.

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:20.159
But while Kafka may not have been able to
escape his abusive father even in death, he

00:22:20.159 --> 00:22:22.659
has now escaped Hermann’s judgement.

00:22:22.659 --> 00:22:28.350
Because history remembers him not as a disappointment,
but as Franz Kafka: the writer who changed

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:47.139
the way we see

00:22:47.139 --> 00:22:50.279
the world.

